Additionally, pursuant to IRS regulations, anytime a debt is charged off, the company is required to issue a Form 1099-C to the consumer so that the consumer can report this information on their taxes as income. I have received the accepted 1099-C copy, which is also considered a certificate of indebtedness according to IRS Publication 4681. Reporting the charged-off debt as income means that it is now considered debt-turned-income. Per IRS guidelines, income is not allowed to be reported on any consumer report. Therefore, I demand that the status of this account be updated to reflect it as "" Paid '' and not as a charge-off.

This account is in violation of 15 U.S. Code 1681a ( d ) ( 2 ) Exclusions, which states that the term "" consumer report '' does not include reports containing information as to transactions or experiences between the consumer and the person making the report. The continued reporting of this account is also a clear violation of 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2, which outlines your responsibilities as a furnisher of information, particularly regarding the accuracy of data reported to consumer reporting agencies.

Additionally, I reference the following applicable laws and regulations : 1. 15 U.S. Code 1681 ( a ) ( 4 ) - Congressional findings and statement of purpose : There is a need to ensure that consumer reporting agencies exercise their grave responsibilities with fairness, impartiality, and respect for the consumer 's right to privacy.

2. 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2 ( a ) ( 1 ) ( B ) - Reporting information after notice and confirmation of errors : A person shall not furnish information relating to a consumer to any consumer reporting agency if the person has been notified by the consumer that specific information is inaccurate, and the information is, in fact, inaccurate.

3. 15 U.S. Code 1681s-2 ( b ) ( 1 ) ( E ) - If an item of information disputed by a consumer is found to be inaccurate or incomplete or can not be verified after any reinvestigation, the furnisher must promptly delete that item of information or permanently block its reporting.

4. 15 U.S. Code 6803 - Disclosure of institution privacy policy : At the time of establishing a customer relationship with a consumer and not less than annually during the continuation of such relationship, a financial institution shall provide a clear and conspicuous disclosure to such consumer, in writing or electronic form, of the institution 's policies and practices with respect to disclosing nonpublic personal information to affiliates and nonaffiliated third parties and protecting the nonpublic personal information of consumers. Failure to comply with these requirements and continued disclosure of my nonpublic information after I ceased being a customer is a direct violation of this law.

5. 15 U.S. Code 1692 ( c ) ( c ) - Cease and desist communication : You are to cease and desist all communication to obtain this alleged debt and terminate all alleged debt collection efforts.

Goldman Sachs Bank, Experian, Equifax and Transunions actions have caused me and my family severe mental, physical & financial stress as well hardship. They all claim to not sell my private information but they do. This is a violation of 15 U.S. Code 1692 ( d ) ( Harassment or abuse ) and 15 U.S. Code 1692 ( f ) ( Unfair practices ). Their actions are also in violation of the Truth in Lending Act ( TILA ), whereby they did not disclose to the consumer ( me ) the right to rescind within 72 hours. 

